        {
          "id": "c14a3e73-c69d-439c-8aec-fb7f14b9578a",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Euclid's Division Lemma",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 3,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "euclid-division-lemma",
          "content_html": "<h3>Euclid's Division Lemma</h3>\n<p>Euclid's Division Lemma is a fundamental theorem in number theory that states:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>For any two positive integers a and b, there exist unique integers q and r such that:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>a = bq + r, where 0 ≤ r < b</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Understanding the Components:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a:</strong> Dividend (the number being divided)</li>\n<li><strong>b:</strong> Divisor (the number dividing)</li>\n<li><strong>q:</strong> Quotient (the result of division)</li>\n<li><strong>r:</strong> Remainder (must be less than divisor)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>For a = 17 and b = 5:</p>\n<p>17 = 5 × 3 + 2</p>\n<p>Here, q = 3 and r = 2 (since 0 ≤ 2 < 5)</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "7ebf7ce9-7854-47f0-b89d-ae2f21f9c9a1",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 4,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "fundamental-theorem-arithmetic",
          "content_html": "<h3>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</h3>\n<p>The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic states that:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Every composite number can be expressed as a unique product of prime numbers, apart from the order of factors.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Key Points:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every number greater than 1 is either prime or can be written as a product of primes</li>\n<li>This factorization is unique (except for the order of factors)</li>\n<li>Prime factorization helps in finding HCF and LCM</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<p>• 12 = 2² × 3</p>\n<p>• 18 = 2 × 3²</p>\n<p>• 30 = 2 × 3 × 5</p>\n<h4>Applications:</h4>\n<p>• Finding HCF: Take the lowest power of each common prime</p>\n<p>• Finding LCM: Take the highest power of each prime</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "1866e9bf-cf6a-4e49-a55c-b0f9c8b773c8",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Introduction to Real Numbers",
          "content": "content 1",
          "lesson_order": 1,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": true,
          "slug": "real-numbers-intro",
          "content_html": "<h3>Understanding Real Numbers</h3>\n<p>Real numbers form the foundation of mathematics. In this lesson, we'll explore:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rational Numbers:</strong> Numbers that can be expressed as fractions (p/q where q ≠ 0)</li>\n<li><strong>Irrational Numbers:</strong> Numbers that cannot be expressed as fractions (like √2, √3, π)</li>\n<li><strong>Properties:</strong> Commutative, associative, and distributive properties</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Key Concepts:</h4>\n<p>Every real number can be represented on the number line. Rational numbers have terminating or repeating decimal expansions, while irrational numbers have non-terminating, non-repeating decimal expansions.</p>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>√2 ≈ 1.414213562... (non-terminating, non-repeating)</p>\n<p>1/3 = 0.333... (repeating decimal)</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "bf1324fb-d3cb-494e-8706-7d0c353bde6b",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Properties of Real Numbers",
          "content": "content 2",
          "lesson_order": 2,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "real-numbers-properties",
          "content_html": "<h3>Properties of Real Numbers</h3>\n<p>Real numbers follow several important properties:</p>\n<h4>1. Commutative Property</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Addition:</strong> a + b = b + a</li>\n<li><strong>Multiplication:</strong> a × b = b × a</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Associative Property</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Addition:</strong> (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)</li>\n<li><strong>Multiplication:</strong> (a × b) × c = a × (b × c)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Distributive Property</h4>\n<p><strong>a × (b + c) = a × b + a × c</strong></p>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<p>• 3 + 5 = 5 + 3 = 8 (Commutative)</p>\n<p>• (2 + 3) + 4 = 2 + (3 + 4) = 9 (Associative)</p>\n<p>• 2 × (3 + 4) = 2 × 3 + 2 × 4 = 14 (Distributive)</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "a1c248c0-44b5-464b-9d09-66c5556c4bb2",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Zeroes of Polynomials",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 6,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "polynomial-zeroes",
          "content_html": "<h3>Zeroes of Polynomials</h3>\n<p>The zeroes (or roots) of a polynomial are the values of the variable that make the polynomial equal to zero.</p>\n<h4>Finding Zeroes:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Linear Polynomial (ax + b):</strong> Zero at x = -b/a</li>\n<li><strong>Quadratic Polynomial (ax² + bx + c):</strong> Use factorization or quadratic formula</li>\n<li><strong>Higher Degree:</strong> Use various methods like factorization, synthetic division</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Relationship between Zeroes and Coefficients:</h4>\n<p>For a quadratic polynomial ax² + bx + c with zeroes α and β:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sum of zeroes:</strong> α + β = -b/a</li>\n<li><strong>Product of zeroes:</strong> α × β = c/a</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<p>• For x² - 5x + 6 = 0:</p>\n<p>  Zeroes: x = 2, x = 3</p>\n<p>  Sum: 2 + 3 = 5 = -(-5)/1</p>\n<p>  Product: 2 × 3 = 6 = 6/1</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "0a5304cd-e701-4ed9-aa78-e55e60cce53d",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Introduction to Linear Equations",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 7,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "linear-equations-intro",
          "content_html": "<h3>Introduction to Linear Equations</h3>\n<p>A linear equation in two variables is an equation of the form ax + by + c = 0, where a, b, and c are real numbers, and a and b are not both zero.</p>\n<h4>General Form:</h4>\n<p><strong>ax + by + c = 0</strong></p>\n<h4>Standard Form:</h4>\n<p><strong>ax + by = c</strong></p>\n<h4>Key Concepts:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Solution:</strong> A pair of values (x, y) that satisfies the equation</li>\n<li><strong>Graph:</strong> A straight line on the coordinate plane</li>\n<li><strong>Consistency:</strong> Whether the system has solutions</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Types of Solutions:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Unique Solution:</strong> Lines intersect at one point</li>\n<li><strong>No Solution:</strong> Lines are parallel</li>\n<li><strong>Infinite Solutions:</strong> Lines coincide</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>2x + 3y = 7</p>\n<p>Solutions: (2, 1), (5, -1), (0, 7/3), etc.</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "ed26468d-010d-424e-b93f-de6225aa4337",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Graphical Method for Linear Equations",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 8,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "graphical-method",
          "content_html": "<h3>Graphical Method for Solving Linear Equations</h3>\n<p>The graphical method involves plotting the equations on a coordinate plane and finding their point of intersection.</p>\n<h4>Steps:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Convert to slope-intercept form:</strong> y = mx + c</li>\n<li><strong>Find two points:</strong> Choose x-values and calculate y-values</li>\n<li><strong>Plot the points:</strong> Draw the line through both points</li>\n<li><strong>Find intersection:</strong> The solution is where lines meet</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Advantages:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Visual representation of the solution</li>\n<li>Easy to understand</li>\n<li>Shows the relationship between variables</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Limitations:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Not precise for decimal solutions</li>\n<li>Difficult for complex systems</li>\n<li>Time-consuming for large problems</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>Solve: x + y = 5 and 2x - y = 1</p>\n<p>Solution: (2, 3)</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "25abc481-0fd3-420f-b118-f149ee2312cb",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Algebraic Methods for Linear Equations",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 9,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "algebraic-methods",
          "content_html": "<h3>Algebraic Methods for Solving Linear Equations</h3>\n<p>There are three main algebraic methods to solve a system of linear equations:</p>\n<h4>1. Substitution Method:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Solve one equation for one variable</li>\n<li>Substitute this expression into the other equation</li>\n<li>Solve for the remaining variable</li>\n<li>Substitute back to find the other variable</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>2. Elimination Method:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Make coefficients of one variable equal</li>\n<li>Add or subtract equations to eliminate that variable</li>\n<li>Solve for the remaining variable</li>\n<li>Substitute back to find the other variable</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>3. Cross Multiplication Method:</h4>\n<p>For equations a₁x + b₁y + c₁ = 0 and a₂x + b₂y + c₂ = 0:</p>\n<p>x/(b₁c₂ - b₂c₁) = y/(c₁a₂ - c₂a₁) = 1/(a₁b₂ - a₂b₁)</p>\n<h4>Example (Substitution):</h4>\n<p>Solve: x + y = 5 and 2x - y = 1</p>\n<p>From first equation: y = 5 - x</p>\n<p>Substitute: 2x - (5 - x) = 1</p>\n<p>Solve: 3x = 6, so x = 2</p>\n<p>Therefore: y = 5 - 2 = 3</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "28869cbf-847b-4382-9fcf-1a559a273c7c",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Introduction to Quadratic Equations",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 10,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "quadratic-intro",
          "content_html": "<h3>Introduction to Quadratic Equations</h3>\n<p>A quadratic equation is a polynomial equation of degree 2, where the highest power of the variable is 2.</p>\n<h4>General Form:</h4>\n<p><strong>ax² + bx + c = 0</strong></p>\n<p>where a ≠ 0, and a, b, c are real numbers.</p>\n<h4>Standard Form:</h4>\n<p><strong>ax² + bx + c = 0</strong></p>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>x² - 5x + 6 = 0 (a=1, b=-5, c=6)</li>\n<li>2x² + 3x - 1 = 0 (a=2, b=3, c=-1)</li>\n<li>x² - 4 = 0 (a=1, b=0, c=-4)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Methods to Solve:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Factorization:</strong> Express as product of linear factors</li>\n<li><strong>Completing the Square:</strong> Convert to perfect square form</li>\n<li><strong>Quadratic Formula:</strong> Direct formula for roots</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Nature of Roots:</h4>\n<p>Depends on the discriminant (b² - 4ac):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>D > 0:</strong> Two distinct real roots</li>\n<li><strong>D = 0:</strong> One real root (repeated)</li>\n<li><strong>D < 0:</strong> No real roots (complex roots)</li>\n</ul>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "2ab2481e-171a-4cf2-99d2-0855a71bc002",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Quadratic Formula",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 11,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "quadratic-formula",
          "content_html": "<h3>Quadratic Formula</h3>\n<p>The quadratic formula provides a direct method to find the roots of any quadratic equation.</p>\n<h4>Formula:</h4>\n<p><strong>x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / 2a</strong></p>\n<h4>Components:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a:</strong> Coefficient of x²</li>\n<li><strong>b:</strong> Coefficient of x</li>\n<li><strong>c:</strong> Constant term</li>\n<li><strong>Discriminant:</strong> b² - 4ac</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Steps:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify a, b, c from the equation</li>\n<li>Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac)</li>\n<li>Substitute values into the formula</li>\n<li>Simplify to get the roots</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Example:</h4>\n<p>Solve: 2x² - 7x + 3 = 0</p>\n<p>a = 2, b = -7, c = 3</p>\n<p>Discriminant = (-7)² - 4(2)(3) = 49 - 24 = 25</p>\n<p>x = (7 ± √25) / 4 = (7 ± 5) / 4</p>\n<p>Therefore: x = 3 or x = 0.5</p>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "6c76cad1-0786-46fb-a756-8a2f38909743",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Quadratic Equations Practice",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 12,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "quadratic-practice",
          "content_html": "<h3>Quadratic Equations Practice Problems</h3>\n<p>This lesson focuses on solving various types of quadratic equations using different methods.</p>\n<h4>Problem Types:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standard Form:</strong> ax² + bx + c = 0</li>\n<li><strong>Missing Terms:</strong> ax² + c = 0 or ax² + bx = 0</li>\n<li><strong>Word Problems:</strong> Real-life applications</li>\n<li><strong>Geometric Problems:</strong> Area, perimeter applications</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Practice Problems:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Solve: x² - 6x + 8 = 0</li>\n<li>Solve: 3x² - 12 = 0</li>\n<li>Solve: 2x² + 5x = 0</li>\n<li>A rectangle has length 5 more than width. If area is 84, find dimensions.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Solutions:</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>x = 2 or x = 4 (factorization)</li>\n<li>x = ±2 (square root method)</li>\n<li>x = 0 or x = -2.5 (factorization)</li>\n<li>Width = 7, Length = 12</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Tips:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Always check your solutions by substitution</li>\n<li>Use the most appropriate method for each problem</li>\n<li>Practice mental math for simple cases</li>\n</ul>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "5f28c3dc-f2c2-4fc9-a5f3-104cd722e091",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "Introduction to Arithmetic Progressions",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 13,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "ap-intro",
          "content_html": "<h3>Introduction to Arithmetic Progressions</h3>\n<p>An Arithmetic Progression (AP) is a sequence of numbers where the difference between consecutive terms is constant.</p>\n<h4>Definition:</h4>\n<p>A sequence a₁, a₂, a₃, ..., aₙ is an AP if:</p>\n<p><strong>a₂ - a₁ = a₃ - a₂ = a₄ - a₃ = ... = d</strong></p>\n<p>where d is called the <strong>common difference</strong>.</p>\n<h4>General Form:</h4>\n<p><strong>a, a + d, a + 2d, a + 3d, ...</strong></p>\n<h4>Key Terms:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First term (a):</strong> The starting value</li>\n<li><strong>Common difference (d):</strong> The constant difference</li>\n<li><strong>nth term (aₙ):</strong> The term at position n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>2, 5, 8, 11, 14, ... (a = 2, d = 3)</li>\n<li>10, 7, 4, 1, -2, ... (a = 10, d = -3)</li>\n<li>3, 3, 3, 3, 3, ... (a = 3, d = 0)</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Applications:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Financial calculations (interest, installments)</li>\n<li>Time and distance problems</li>\n<li>Pattern recognition</li>\n</ul>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        },
        {
          "id": "aba1e756-7797-40ba-8fc9-506f7a9573c7",
          "course_id": "60070fd5-e847-46d2-8786-94c8022f8682",
          "title": "AP Formulas and Properties",
          "content": null,
          "lesson_order": 14,
          "created_at": "2025-09-10T14:00:59.317226+00:00",
          "is_preview": false,
          "slug": "ap-formulas",
          "content_html": "<h3>AP Formulas and Properties</h3>\n<p>This lesson covers the essential formulas for working with Arithmetic Progressions.</p>\n<h4>1. nth Term Formula:</h4>\n<p><strong>aₙ = a + (n - 1)d</strong></p>\n<h4>2. Sum of First n Terms:</h4>\n<p><strong>Sₙ = n/2 [2a + (n - 1)d]</strong></p>\n<p>or</p>\n<p><strong>Sₙ = n/2 [a + aₙ]</strong></p>\n<h4>3. Sum of Terms from mth to nth:</h4>\n<p><strong>Sₘ₋ₙ = (n - m + 1)/2 [aₘ + aₙ]</strong></p>\n<h4>Properties:</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Three consecutive terms:</strong> If a, b, c are in AP, then 2b = a + c</li>\n<li><strong>Arithmetic Mean:</strong> AM = (a + b)/2</li>\n<li><strong>Sum of equidistant terms:</strong> Equal from beginning and end</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Examples:</h4>\n<p>For AP: 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, ... (a = 3, d = 4)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>10th term: a₁₀ = 3 + (10-1)×4 = 39</li>\n<li>Sum of first 10 terms: S₁₀ = 10/2 [2×3 + 9×4] = 210</li>\n</ul>",
          "content_text": null,
          "content_type": "html"
        }
      ]
    },
